Dear Jennifer,

I have recorded the engineering assessments and dispositions for QSCP-NCR-2024-031 and QSCP-NCR-2024-032 within the ATU-100. For 4-RA-101, continued operation is permitted pending PAUT mapping and a fitness-for-service assessment. Regarding 2-RF-102, the degradation is identified as localized velocity-assisted ammonium bisulfide corrosion downstream of the reflux flow-control valve, resulting from the 2014 throughput increase. A reflux-water sample confirmed 3 wt% NH4HS, and XRF PMI verified the material as 316 stainless steel.

Under QSCP-CIS-001, the assessment for 2-RF-102 confirms the minimum wall of 1.9 mm is well above the B31.3 pressure-design thickness of 0.2 mm. With the addition of a support at the valve station, the structural limit is 1.3 mm. At a corrosion rate of 0.18 mm/y, the line will remain above this limit until after the August 2026 turnaround. The disposition requires a 12-month re-measurement and full replacement of the downstream section during the 2026 turnaround, or sooner if projected by the next survey.

Best regards,  Robert Hendricks Jennifer Walsh

